    <h1>Hrafn: The Old Norse Word for Raven</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> <em>Hrafn</em> is the Old Norse word for the Common Raven (<em>Corvus corax</em>). It descends from Proto-Germanic *<em>hrabnaz</em>, related to Old English <em>hrǣfn</em> (modern English "raven"), Old High German <em>hraban</em>, and Gothic <em>hrabns</em>. The word appears throughout Old Norse poetry and prose — in the names of Odin's ravens (Huginn and Muninn), in personal names, and in dozens of place-names across Scandinavia and Iceland.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Etymology — From Proto-Germanic to Modern Languages</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The Proto-Germanic root *<em>hrabnaz</em> (raven) is reconstructed by linguists from the parallel forms found across early Germanic languages:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Old Norse: <em>hrafn</em></li>
      <li>Old English: <em>hrǣfn</em> → Middle English <em>raven</em> → Modern English "raven"</li>
      <li>Old High German: <em>hraban</em>, <em>raban</em></li>
      <li>Old Saxon: <em>hraban</em></li>
      <li>Gothic: <em>hrabns</em></li>
    </ul>
    <p>The word is not etymologically connected to Latin <em>corvus</em> (which gives the scientific genus name <em>Corvus</em>). The Germanic branch and the Latin branch are parallel, not derived from each other.</p>
    <p>The root is ultimately onomatopoeic — it reflects the deep, croaking call of the raven. Compare the similar principle in Latin <em>corvus</em>, which is likewise derived from the raven's call (from the verb <em>crocire</em>, to croak).</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Hrafn in Old Norse Poetry and Mythology</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The word <em>hrafn</em> appears frequently in Old Norse eddic and skaldic poetry, most famously in the names of Odin's two ravens:<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Huginn</strong> (<em>hugr</em> + suffix — "thought/mind") — Odin's raven of thought</li>
      <li><strong>Muninn</strong> (<em>munr</em> + suffix — "memory/mind") — Odin's raven of memory</li>
    </ul>
    <p>See <a href="/lore/norse/huginn-and-muninn/">Huginn and Muninn — full chapter</a> for the complete account.</p>
    <p>Beyond Huginn and Muninn, the word <em>hrafn</em> serves as the base for a cluster of important kennings (compound metaphors) in skaldic poetry — see <a href="/lore/norse/ravens-in-eddic-kennings/">Ravens in eddic kennings</a>.</p>
    <p>Odin's title <em>Hrafnaguð</em> ("Raven-God" or "God of the Raven") encapsulates the intimate connection between Odin and the raven in Norse religion — see <a href="/lore/norse/odin-the-raven-god/">Odin the Raven-God</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Hrafn as a Personal Name</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> <em>Hrafn</em> was a common personal name in Old Norse-speaking Scandinavia and Iceland. The <em>Landnámabók</em> (Book of Settlements, recording Iceland's settlement in the 9th–10th centuries) mentions several settlers named Hrafn, and raven-names appear as both primary names and elements of compound names throughout the sagas.<sup><a href="#src-3">3</a></sup></p>
    <p>Notable saga characters with raven-names include:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Hrafnkell</strong> — the protagonist of <em>Hrafnkels saga Freysgoða</em> (Hrafnkell's Saga), one of the best-known Icelandic sagas. His name means roughly "Raven-Kettle" (the second element <em>-ketill</em> was a common name component).</li>
      <li><strong>Flóki Vilgerðarson</strong> — known as <em>Hrafna-Flóki</em> (Raven-Flóki), who used ravens to navigate to Iceland. See <a href="/lore/norse/raven-floki-and-iceland/">Raven-Flóki and Iceland</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Place-Names Containing Hrafn</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Raven place-names are abundant across the Norse world, reflecting both the bird's ecological importance and its cultural prestige. Examples include:<sup><a href="#src-3">3</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Iceland:</strong> Hrafnseyri (a farm in the Westfjords where the independence leader Jón Sigurðsson was born); Hrafnagjá (a gorge near Þingvellir)</li>
      <li><strong>Norway:</strong> numerous <em>Ravn-</em> prefixed farms and geographic features</li>
      <li><strong>Orkney and Shetland:</strong> <em>Rafn-</em> place-names reflecting Norse settlement</li>
      <li><strong>England:</strong> place-names with the Old English cognate <em>hrǣfn</em> occur in areas of heavy Norse/Anglo-Saxon presence — Ravensthorpe, Ravenscar, Ravenswood</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Bird Behind the Word</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Common Raven (<em>Corvus corax</em>) is a year-round resident across Scandinavia and Iceland — ubiquitous on farmland, coast, and mountain alike. Its visibility and behaviour (following hunters, scavenging battlefields, its intelligence and its call) would have made it familiar to Norse-speaking people in a way that shapes the depth of its cultural presence. See <a href="/species/common-raven/">Common Raven — species profile</a>.</p>
  </section>

